The financial implications of managing conditions like phenylketonuria are substantial, with healthcare costs for home artificial nutrition in Europe ranging from $75,000 to $122,000 per patient per year. These costs underscore the importance of effective dietary management and the potential for cost reductions through improved health outcomes and preventive measures [Data: Entities (16002); Relationships (11375)].# Clinical Trials and Regulatory Framework in Spain

