The Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) community is centered around a set of protocols designed to improve recovery outcomes for patients undergoing elective surgeries. Key entities include ERAS itself, various surgical procedures, and countries contributing to the initiative, such as Norway, Scotland, and the Netherlands. The relationships among these entities highlight the importance of nutritional support, multidisciplinary integration, and the focus on reducing morbidity and hospitalization costs.

## ERAS as a comprehensive recovery protocol

Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) is a comprehensive set of recommendations aimed at improving recovery for patients undergoing elective surgery. The program focuses on multimodal rehabilitation protocols that enhance patient outcomes by addressing various aspects of perioperative care. This includes nutritional support, pain management, and early mobilization, which are critical for optimizing recovery processes. The implementation of ERAS protocols has been shown to reduce length of hospital stays and associated costs, making it a vital component of modern surgical practices [Data: Entities (12584, 12583); Relationships (19196, 11388)].

## The role of nutrition in ERAS

Nutrition plays a pivotal role in the ERAS protocols, emphasizing the importance of nutritional support for improving surgical outcomes. The ERAS recommendations include the use of enteral nutrition to enhance recovery post-surgery, particularly for patients at risk of malnutrition. This focus on nutrition is crucial, especially for older adults who may experience a decline in muscle mass during hospitalization. By addressing nutritional needs early in the hospitalization process, ERAS aims to optimize recovery and reduce complications [Data: Entities (12594, 12592); Relationships (2789, 3755)].

## International collaboration in ERAS

Norway, Scotland, and the Netherlands are among the northern European countries that have significantly contributed to the establishment and development of the ERAS initiative. These countries have collaborated to create standardized protocols that enhance surgical recovery, demonstrating a commitment to improving health outcomes on an international scale. Norway's involvement in data collection for the WHO's international growth reference patterns further underscores its role in advancing global health standards [Data: Entities (12586, 12584); Relationships (19194, 19193, 19195)].

## Impact of ERAS on morbidity

The ERAS program aims to reduce morbidity associated with surgical procedures by implementing evidence-based practices that enhance recovery. By focusing on factors such as pain management, nutrition, and early mobilization, ERAS protocols seek to minimize complications and improve overall patient health. This focus on reducing morbidity is particularly important for high-risk surgical patients, as it can lead to better long-term outcomes and quality of life [Data: Entities (12593, 12592); Relationships (19201)].

