Primary amenorrhea, defined as the absence of menstruation by age 15, can be caused by various hormonal disorders, including Turner Syndrome and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. The relationship between primary amenorrhea and these conditions highlights the importance of hormonal evaluation in affected individuals. For instance, patients with Turner Syndrome often experience primary amenorrhea due to insufficient ovarian function, necessitating hormone replacement therapy to address developmental and reproductive challenges. [Data: Entities (5308); Relationships (12814, 12815)]

